.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt MAC_CAPABILITY_CAPPROTECT 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mac_capability_capprotect
.Nd MAC_CAPABILITY capability protection service
.Sh SYNOPSIS
The service is compiled into every 5BSD kernel as part of the mandatory
.Xr mac_capability 4
plane; it is not a loadable module.
.Pp
Wire protocol header:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <dev/mac_capability/mac_capability_capprotect_proto.h>
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
service provides selective per-process integrity protection using the
MACF framework.
A process shields itself by calling
.Dv CP_OP_SHIELD
with a bitmask of desired protections, or a launcher that holds a process
descriptor for a child it created with
.Xr pdfork 2
shields that child by calling
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
with the descriptor attached.
.Pp
A shield is bound to the protected process (its PID and a per-shield
generation number), not to a program nonce.
It persists across
.Xr execve 2
and across closing the capability fd that applied it, and is removed only
when the protected process exits.
Forked children of a shielded process are not shielded; they must install
their own protection, or their launcher must protect them.
Repeated
.Dv CP_OP_SHIELD
or
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
calls on the same process add flags to the existing shield.
.Pp
Three parties may interact with a shielded process regardless of its shield:
the process itself; the
.Em protector ,
the process that applied the shield
(the process itself for
.Dv CP_OP_SHIELD ,
the launcher for
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT ) ,
which retains lifecycle access such as signalling and waiting; and any
process that has activated an access token minted for that shield.
Fork siblings and other processes sharing the program nonce receive no
implicit authority.
.Pp
The holder of a shield instance grants access to another process by
minting an access token
.Pq Dv CP_OP_MINT
and handing it over.
The recipient activates the token with
.Dv CP_OP_AUTHORIZE
to gain access through the shield for the flags the token carries.
Tokens name a shield incarnation; a token minted for a shield whose process
has since exited is useless even if the PID is reused.
Authorization persists while the token fd is open, and each token admits
exactly one accessor.
Closing the token fd revokes authorization.
.Pp
All operations use
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL
(synchronous).
.Ss Shield flags
Flags fall in two groups.
Protection flags block what foreign processes may do to the shielded
process; restriction flags limit what the shielded process may do itself.
.Bl -tag -width CP_SF_NOFDRECV
.It Dv CP_SF_PTRACE Pq 0x0001
Block
.Xr ptrace 2
attach from foreign processes.
.It Dv CP_SF_SIGNAL Pq 0x0002
Block signals from foreign processes, and suspension that bypasses signal
delivery.
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGCONT
require their own flags.
.It Dv CP_SF_VISIBLE Pq 0x0004
Accepted for wire compatibility only.
Process visibility is not enforced in the per-process model because the
visibility hook is credential-to-credential and cannot identify the
shielded target; the flag has no effect.
.It Dv CP_SF_WAIT Pq 0x0008
Block
.Xr wait4 2
from foreign processes.
The real parent may always wait for its child.
.It Dv CP_SF_SIGKILL Pq 0x0010
Block
.Dv SIGKILL
from foreign processes.
Makes the process unkillable except by itself, its protector, or authorized
holders.
.It Dv CP_SF_SIGCONT Pq 0x0020
Block
.Dv SIGCONT
from foreign processes.
.It Dv CP_SF_SCHED Pq 0x0040
Block
.Xr getpriority 2 ,
.Xr setpriority 2 ,
and
.Xr cpuset 2
manipulation from foreign processes.
.It Dv CP_SF_CORE Pq 0x0080
Suppress core dumps for the protected process.
.It Dv CP_SF_KTRACE Pq 0x0100
Block
.Xr ktrace 1
from foreign processes.
.It Dv CP_SF_NOPRIVS Pq 0x0200
Strip all privileges from the protected process:
every
.Xr priv 9
check fails with
.Er EPERM .
.It Dv CP_SF_NOFORK Pq 0x0400
Block
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr vfork 2 ,
.Xr rfork 2 ,
and
.Xr pdfork 2 .
.It Dv CP_SF_NOIPC Pq 0x0800
Block SysV shared memory, semaphore and message queue operations and POSIX
shared memory and semaphore opens.
.It Dv CP_SF_NOFDRECV Pq 0x1000
Block incoming
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
file descriptor transfers over local-domain sockets.
This flag removes ambient descriptor receipt; it does not block attachments
received over a previously held
.Xr mac_capability_channel 4
endpoint.
Such attachments remain subject to the channel's explicit authority,
descriptor-transfer limit, close-on-exec state, and close-on-fork state.
.It Dv CP_SF_NOEXEC Pq 0x2000
Block
.Xr execve 2 .
.It Dv CP_SF_NOSOCK Pq 0x4000
Block creation of new sockets with
.Xr socket 2 .
.It Dv CP_SF_PROTECT Pq 0x01ff
All protection flags:
.Dv CP_SF_PTRACE ,
.Dv CP_SF_SIGNAL ,
.Dv CP_SF_VISIBLE ,
.Dv CP_SF_WAIT ,
.Dv CP_SF_SIGKILL ,
.Dv CP_SF_SIGCONT ,
.Dv CP_SF_SCHED ,
.Dv CP_SF_CORE ,
and
.Dv CP_SF_KTRACE .
.It Dv CP_SF_RESTRICT Pq 0x7e00
All restriction flags:
.Dv CP_SF_NOPRIVS ,
.Dv CP_SF_NOFORK ,
.Dv CP_SF_NOIPC ,
.Dv CP_SF_NOFDRECV ,
.Dv CP_SF_NOEXEC ,
and
.Dv CP_SF_NOSOCK .
.It Dv CP_SF_ALL Pq 0x7fff
All protections and restrictions.
.El
.Pp
Passing
.Fa flags Ns = Ns 0
to
.Dv CP_OP_SHIELD
or
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
selects
.Dv CP_SF_ALL ,
including the self-restrictions.
.Ss Operations
.Bl -tag -width CP_OP_AUTHORIZE
.It Dv CP_OP_SHIELD Pq 1
Shield the calling process with the specified flags.
The caller becomes its own protector.
The instance fd becomes an active shield instance from which tokens may be
minted; closing it does not remove the shield.
.It Dv CP_OP_MINT Pq 2
Create an access token (returned as a reply fd) for the shield this
instance applied.
.Fa flags
selects the subset of the instance's flags the token conveys; zero means all
of them.
Only available on an active shield instance whose target process is still
alive.
.It Dv CP_OP_AUTHORIZE Pq 3
Called on a token fd.
Authorizes the calling process to interact with the shielded target
for the token's flags despite the shield.
A token admits one accessor; a repeated call by the same accessor succeeds
without effect.
.It Dv CP_OP_CAPMODE Pq 4
Enter Capsicum capability mode for the calling process.
Idempotent if the process is already in capability mode.
.It Dv CP_OP_CHROOT Pq 5
Change the filesystem root of the calling process.
The new root directory fd is attached to the message.
.It Dv CP_OP_PROTECT Pq 6
Shield the process named by an attached process descriptor
(see
.Xr procdesc 4 )
with the specified flags.
The caller becomes the target's protector and retains the lifecycle access
described above; the target itself need not participate.
This is how
.Xr switchboard 8
applies a unit manifest's
.Va protect
list to a freshly forked service before it executes, and how
.Xr capsule 8
shields
.Xr switchboard 8 .
The process descriptor must still be transferable when the call is made.
Like
.Dv CP_OP_SHIELD ,
the instance becomes an active shield instance for the target and may mint
tokens for it.
.El
.Ss Wire protocol
.Bd -literal
#include <dev/mac_capability/mac_capability_capprotect_proto.h>

struct cp_request {
    uint32_t op;      /* CP_OP_* */
    uint32_t flags;   /* CP_SF_* bitmask for SHIELD/PROTECT/MINT, 0 for others */
} __packed;
.Ed
.Pp
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
attaches one process descriptor and
.Dv CP_OP_CHROOT
one directory descriptor as the first request descriptor.
The reply uses the standard
.Xr mac_capability 4
call reply mechanism: an empty reply for success.
For
.Dv CP_OP_MINT ,
the reply fd carries the new access token; the caller must supply a reply
descriptor slot.
.Ss MACF hooks gated
Foreign access to a shielded process is denied with
.Er EACCES
by the following hooks when the corresponding flag is set:
.Fn mpo_proc_check_debug
.Pq Dv CP_SF_PTRACE ,
.Fn mpo_proc_check_signal
.Pq Dv CP_SF_SIGNAL , CP_SF_SIGKILL , CP_SF_SIGCONT ,
.Fn mpo_proc_check_suspend
.Pq Dv CP_SF_SIGNAL ,
.Fn mpo_proc_check_wait
.Pq Dv CP_SF_WAIT ,
.Fn mpo_proc_check_sched
.Pq Dv CP_SF_SCHED ,
and
.Fn mpo_proc_check_ktrace
.Pq Dv CP_SF_KTRACE .
.Fn mpo_proc_check_core
denies the dump with
.Er EPERM
.Pq Dv CP_SF_CORE .
.Pp
Self-restrictions are enforced on the shielded process itself by
.Fn mpo_priv_check
.Pq Dv CP_SF_NOPRIVS , Er EPERM ,
.Fn mpo_proc_check_fork
.Pq Dv CP_SF_NOFORK , Er EPERM ,
.Fn mpo_vnode_check_exec
.Pq Dv CP_SF_NOEXEC , Er EPERM ,
.Fn mpo_socket_check_create
.Pq Dv CP_SF_NOSOCK , Er EPERM ,
.Fn mpo_file_check_receive
.Pq Dv CP_SF_NOFDRECV , Er EACCES ,
and the SysV shm/sem/msq and POSIX shm/sem hooks
.Pq Dv CP_SF_NOIPC , Er EACCES .
.Pp
When no process on the system is shielded, every hook returns immediately
without taking a lock.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va kern.mac_capability_capprotect.max_auth
Maximum number of outstanding token authorizations (loader tunable;
0 = unlimited).
.Dv CP_OP_AUTHORIZE
fails with
.Er ENOSPC
once the limit is reached.
.It Va kern.mac_capability_capprotect.auth_count
Current number of authorizations (read-only).
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /dev/mac_capability
The
.Xr mac_capability 4
device node used by
.Fn mac_capability_connect .
.El
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The following DTrace probes are available under the
.Nm mac_capability_capprotect
provider:
.Bl -tag -width "state"
.It Li "deny(reason, target_pid, accessor_pid)"
Fires when a shield denies an operation from a foreign process, or a
self-restriction denies the shielded process.
.It Li "allow(reason, target_pid, accessor_pid)"
Fires when a protected operation is allowed (protector or valid token).
.It Li "state(operation, target, caller, flags, pid, error)"
Fires on a state change:
.Dq shield , protect , token-mint , authorize , capmode ,
.Dq chroot , chroot-error
or
.Dq token-remove .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Shield a process from ptrace and signals:
.Bd -literal
int fd = mac_capability_connect("capprotect");
struct cp_request req = { .op = CP_OP_SHIELD,
    .flags = CP_SF_PTRACE | CP_SF_SIGNAL };
struct mac_capability_call_args ca = { .req = &req, .req_len = sizeof(req) };
ioctl(fd, MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL, &ca);
/* Protected until the process exits */
.Ed
.Pp
Protect a child from its launcher, before the child executes:
.Bd -literal
int pd_fd;
pid_t pid = pdfork(&pd_fd, PD_CLOEXEC);
if (pid == 0) {
	/* child: wait for the go-ahead, then execve() */
}
struct cp_request req = { .op = CP_OP_PROTECT,
    .flags = CP_SF_PROTECT };
struct mac_capability_call_args ca = { .req = &req, .req_len = sizeof(req),
    .req_fds = &pd_fd, .req_nfds = 1 };
ioctl(capprotect_fd, MAC_CAPABILITY_CALL, &ca);
/* The launcher may still pdkill(pd_fd, ...) and pdwait the child */
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
Operations may fail with the following errors:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Invalid operation request length; flags outside
.Dv CP_SF_ALL ;
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
without an attached process descriptor;
.Dv CP_OP_CHROOT
without an attached vnode descriptor;
.Dv CP_OP_SHIELD ,
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
or
.Dv CP_OP_MINT
issued on a token fd;
.Dv CP_OP_MINT
on an instance that has not applied a shield, requesting flags the instance
does not hold, or without a reply descriptor slot;
or
.Dv CP_OP_AUTHORIZE
on a non-token fd.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The target of
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
has already exited, or the shield a token or instance refers to no longer
exists because its process exited.
.It Bq Er EALREADY
.Dv CP_OP_AUTHORIZE
on a token that another process has already activated.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The
.Va kern.mac_capability_capprotect.max_auth
limit was reached.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The shield generation counter is exhausted.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
The descriptor attached to
.Dv CP_OP_CHROOT
is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
Unknown operation code.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
The capability fd lacks the required rights for the operation.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr mac 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_channel 4 ,
.Xr procdesc 4 ,
.Xr capsule 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
service first appeared in 5BSD.
The per-process shield model and launcher-applied
.Dv CP_OP_PROTECT
appeared in 5BSD 16.0.
